#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105612 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105760 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba3182f3-3b70-5d65-bbb3-6a5e163b3a1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection of Two Circles Where One Is Centered at the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "e17be858-3226-5845-b0c9-f3cf86987ee6" :question/math-academy-id "e-5395" :question/problem "The circles ![](images/62/62bff9ae4f0299ac4cd10261ad85c8b0d7e5e39b6ea5e779eead290e4c5bba38.svg) and ![](images/8b/8b994afa2154f42fc39a361c03eb163e6ae83e588ebbe949028a0bc75e10b16d.svg)  intersect at points ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) and ![](images/65/65ada9c919fe5c4bee19fb4cafff28ed46a36bf6fc90b4cecf70e0fa02386ce0.svg) as shown below. What are the polar coordinates of the point ![](images/49/499b8f6de313125b2cf1bbd2ab7aa4c1044f542237f5abdf31b877ce5aecdaa6.svg) \n\n![](images/5d/5d7d5084d0b13c7484acdc8796bcd8584d77351befacacf84a153430b34f6fd4.png)" :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for ![](images/3a/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) and ![](images/90/907cfc4c25fd53cc279e07e970a8e68fab64a3558b575e0b3dfe1bd9cda24ccd.svg)\n![](images/95/952b380a0e3dc52afe15e5cd8799eb50cb4125b726a1d540436fca0e75894d0e.svg)\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solving for ![](images/b9/b98533dee87a7bd8ed4d593a2aabd517621d6ab96e40a5b2ab4a8ac1923ddac5.svg) This gives\n\n![](images/ea/ea2a71a3cb39086a94ea65088c951dbe8c0ec542b224f3bb724d8cbf27416f06.svg)\n\nSolving this equation in the interval ![](images/b5/b501be2da8128f39c033d09dc0e2fc423e4df98c845acbdee221efbaa5cc6978.svg) we obtain  ![](images/4f/4ffc15045d598404d6a76b86de375abc74e21c66821149760272d73eca6b3190.svg) and ![](images/2a/2a50ea8c8351cbd27e82b2c20da9a482d093b20e039a650930857b36700968b1.svg)\n\nAlso, notice that ![](images/8b/8b994afa2154f42fc39a361c03eb163e6ae83e588ebbe949028a0bc75e10b16d.svg) at both intersection points.\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the point ![](images/75/7533ad047439b7d799dd7afee5d6a7bc9f4404fbadb8c3f27ef6d3952119ff6b.svg) are ![](images/ee/eece868ab8c5a66a3f640d9f98b6ce38ed8659c0eef8158a55c5ce4edc1a5157.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105614 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105762 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64910a32-96b4-59c4-8f61-84e7c532e59d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection of a Limaçon and Another Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "d58288cb-2983-5927-8436-8dbd39d6f67d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5397" :question/problem "Find the polar coordinates of the intersection points of the polar curves  $r =3+3\\cos\\theta$ and $r = 4,$ shown below. Round your answers to $1$ decimal place where appropriate.\n\n![](images/cb/cbdc7d93d48f21d3d4ae67a5cb7692801d240e896263a137557e67f8a5bfbcfc.png)" :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=3+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solving for $\\theta.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t3+3\\cos \\theta & = 4\\[5pt]\n \t \t3\\cos \\theta & = 1   \\[3pt]\n \t\t\\cos \\theta & = \\dfrac 1 3.\n\t}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0^\\circ,360^\\circ),$ we obtain $\\theta_1 \\approx 70.5^{\\circ}$ and $\\theta_2 \\approx 289.5,$ rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\nNotice that we must have \n$r=4$\n at any of the points of intersection.\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n(r,\\theta)=\\left(4 , 70.5 \\, ^\\circ \\right)\\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad (r,\\theta)=\\left(4 , 289.5 \\, ^\\circ\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105615 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105763 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "179ed9f0-7568-5cb0-8f06-626e1e494b62" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection Between a Circle and Another Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "716ee2b8-17d2-5754-a09d-cec35808df9d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5399" :question/problem "The circles $r=4\\cos \\theta$ and $r=4\\sqrt{3}\\sin  \\theta$ intersect at points $O$ and $A,$ as shown in the diagram. What are the polar coordinates of the point $A?$\n\n![](images/27/2766090535f1931a0cb15a248eb62c9b1dc5b5f9bc15b6dff5d544876527d77c.png)" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we notice from the graph that $(r,\\theta)=\\left(0, 0\\right)$ is an obvious first point of intersection. \n\nTo find the second point of intersection, we set the equations of the curves equal to each other and solve for $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{4}{4\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the solution $\\theta =\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ in the $1$st quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=4\\cos \\theta$ to find the $r$-coordinate of the point $A\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(r,\\theta)=\\left(2\\sqrt{3}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105760 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba3182f3-3b70-5d65-bbb3-6a5e163b3a1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection of Two Circles Where One Is Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25fa1f4b-97ef-5790-af1e-168d7931db47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,{30}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545280} {:answer/id #uuid "6b601d25-f8de-5b76-b357-7333dea0a6be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},{330}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545402} {:answer/id #uuid "70730940-311e-5ac8-82e7-b732c8d7b04b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,{120}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545411} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8446d6-878c-53e5-8dc5-9f23ed0b752e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,{135}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545463} {:answer/id #uuid "f603c36e-3fc5-5979-b39d-11d91b66908a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},{135}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e8446d6-878c-53e5-8dc5-9f23ed0b752e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,{135}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545463} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e5baca4-797a-5167-9991-36c46ba93ec2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8d90f0eb-b4e6-5cf1-abd5-55559dbeabf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-120748" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f78441133b6c3ac196e9e9e8dcfbbbdef875468e54ee34a3509b26d7ed86578e.png)\n\nThe circles $r=2$ and $r=2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ intersect at points $A$ and $B,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates of the point $B?$" :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solving for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[{0}^{∘},\\text{ }\\,{360}^{∘}),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}={45}^{∘}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}={135}^{∘}.$ \n\nAlso, notice that $r=2$ at both intersection points.\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the point $B$ are \n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(2,{135}^{∘}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105760 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba3182f3-3b70-5d65-bbb3-6a5e163b3a1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection of Two Circles Where One Is Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fd77f70-e1a1-50cc-801e-9246f14fe6dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,{330}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545320} {:answer/id #uuid "45d2dac0-5bd8-5fa3-81ab-7b8d2b12b311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},{330}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545333} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3b5e0b-fabc-5514-a006-5d26116b1cea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,{300}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545541} {:answer/id #uuid "bd1993ad-2046-5bbf-b084-3d5a7f7e6622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,{300}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545543} {:answer/id #uuid "de96cb1d-479d-57f8-8221-6b3c0e495910" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,{315}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd1993ad-2046-5bbf-b084-3d5a7f7e6622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,{300}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545543} :answer-field/id #uuid "dedbb997-f316-5f26-8b09-344ac2060a51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47f83011-f0ac-5fe7-b577-1abb06b9a992" :question/math-academy-id "q-121755" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f556adb5f1af77ed89db28638a19051b6ebb8ff056eabe60a8a6357984011a12.png)\n\nThe circles $r=1$ and $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ intersect at points $A$ and $B,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates of the point $B?$" :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solving for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[{0}^{∘},\\text{ }\\,{360}^{∘}),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}={60}^{∘}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}={300}^{∘}.$  \n\nAlso, notice that $r=1$ at both intersection points.\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the point $B$ are \n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(1,{300}^{∘}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105763 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "179ed9f0-7568-5cb0-8f06-626e1e494b62" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection Between a Circle and Another Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "287221ce-e666-58c8-b087-6ba9c8f66683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},{315}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545287} {:answer/id #uuid "2d98655d-e11c-5e52-8f86-862670c63ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},{330}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545294} {:answer/id #uuid "50395f2a-ef0b-5443-adfd-eb8d58b5b772" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},{300}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545350} {:answer/id #uuid "83ca30d5-bddc-58c6-8bb1-02f885a76084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,{330}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545447} {:answer/id #uuid "e4068d4b-2d54-5c8e-b63e-f5f8c260fc9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,{315}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "287221ce-e666-58c8-b087-6ba9c8f66683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},{315}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545287} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b8e7f42-8da1-5cc1-8274-3f4351f120b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a5a55304-1327-53a7-ab60-6725a01ed1fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-121800" :question/problem "![](images/9e/9e7a57e93473508aa49868e75769d905f52be9b3b6c74bfd9328b5e9bfe240c7.png)\n\nThe circles $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $r=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ intersect at points $O$ and $A,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates of the point $A?$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we notice from the graph that the origin $O$ is an obvious first point of intersection.\n\nTo find the second point of intersection, we set the equations of the curves equal to each other and solve for $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =-\\frac{2}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the solution $\\theta ={315}^{∘}$ in the $4$th quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinate of the point $A:$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(\\sqrt{2},{315}^{∘}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545861 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105762 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64910a32-96b4-59c4-8f61-84e7c532e59d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection of a Limaçon and Another Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1489df57-7fc5-588f-96d3-c120cf10a7d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3.0,{197.5}^{∘})$ and $(3.0,{342.5}^{∘})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545237} {:answer/id #uuid "20e83cd9-7cc6-5c6f-ac11-378a2d41900d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3.4,{191.5}^{∘})$ and $(3.4,{348.5}^{∘})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545265} {:answer/id #uuid "570d7b76-2255-5c9a-bbf6-0130f9b43c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3.1,{189.6}^{∘})$ and $(3.1,{350.4}^{∘})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545362} {:answer/id #uuid "5dba6239-f541-5546-9927-92a968513a5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3.5,{202.4}^{∘})$ and $(3.5,{337.6}^{∘})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545378} {:answer/id #uuid "6b6e0900-8fbd-5105-8ac6-daf150f9d1cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3.2,{194.3}^{∘})$ and $(3.2,{345.7}^{∘})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20e83cd9-7cc6-5c6f-ac11-378a2d41900d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3.4,{191.5}^{∘})$ and $(3.4,{348.5}^{∘})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545265} :answer-field/id #uuid "883b05a3-cd95-5802-ba49-1d8e57c33615" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cdda3bdc-973b-5424-bda2-1798eb90cfd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-121827" :question/problem "![](images/ca/caabcee019c7e97355b6a30f820872207e3f43245d97d3d09a44bf400628a975.png)\n\nFind the polar coordinates of the intersection points of the polar curves $r=3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=4+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ shown above. Round your answers to $1$ decimal place where appropriate." :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=4+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =4+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}≈{191.5}^{∘}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}≈348.5,$ rounded to $1$ decimal place. \n\nNow, we use the equation $r=4+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinates of the intersection points: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1} & =4+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{1}=4+3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5})=\\frac{17}{5}=3.4 \\\\ {r}_{2} & =4+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{2}=4+3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5})=\\frac{17}{5}=3.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the points of intersection are\n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )≈(3.4,{191.5}^{∘})\\,$ and $\\,(r,\\theta )≈(3.4,{348.5}^{∘}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105762 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64910a32-96b4-59c4-8f61-84e7c532e59d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection of a Limaçon and Another Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b8a8462-9d74-596a-9456-548160d90875" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,{30}^{∘})$ and $(2,{150}^{∘})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545431} {:answer/id #uuid "7df40776-420d-5e36-a8f3-ab3b8b4797bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,{60}^{∘})$ and $(2,{120}^{∘})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545432} {:answer/id #uuid "81d95ec4-26fc-557f-81bd-a82a364c0ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,{30}^{∘})$ and $(1,{150}^{∘})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545441} {:answer/id #uuid "991f7fc3-fdd7-51ab-9099-ff9096589f5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,{45}^{∘})$ and $(1,{135}^{∘})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545476} {:answer/id #uuid "a44f1b45-7e6e-5e5c-ba1f-31f87526a464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,{60}^{∘})$ and $(1,{120}^{∘})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81d95ec4-26fc-557f-81bd-a82a364c0ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,{30}^{∘})$ and $(1,{150}^{∘})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545441} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa11ec00-ff11-56ba-9564-ddc523852282" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545753}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92072883-4631-52cf-ad3b-532b194d125c" :question/math-academy-id "q-121831" :question/problem "![](images/b8/b80f9b57f7ed0e79a9ebbe387d5cedc1a46e23da2d498599bc48ee52c76ff190.png)\n\nFind the polar coordinates of the intersection points of the polar curves  $r=2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=1,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,r & =2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\,r & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}={30}^{∘}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}={150}^{∘}.$  \n\nNotice that we must have $r=1$ for any point that lies on both curves. \n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the points of intersection are\n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(1,{30}^{∘})\\,$ and $\\,(r,\\theta )=(1,{150}^{∘}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105762 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64910a32-96b4-59c4-8f61-84e7c532e59d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection of a Limaçon and Another Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5748d238-2d62-5c05-9b32-cc45a9cb23cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{4\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545363} {:answer/id #uuid "7a34109a-a5f7-5e0d-9dea-2408d0c9fe14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545430} {:answer/id #uuid "9b661e8b-5a92-5a04-a768-7616735c352b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545479} {:answer/id #uuid "c4277d9a-1776-504e-a740-20236a450250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545554} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e6e99c-04e1-5531-ac7d-3bc194756e95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{2\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4277d9a-1776-504e-a740-20236a450250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545554} :answer-field/id #uuid "00c32dba-43b3-51e4-90b4-f83a74a787dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b30c011d-e3fe-5a6f-9c15-6828aaa923bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-121848" :question/problem "The curves $r=2-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ intersect at a single point in the second quadrant. Determine the polar coordinates of this point." :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=2-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0,2\\pi ),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the $\\theta $-coordinate of the point that we're interested in is $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$ since it lies in the second quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinate of our point:\n\n$$\nr=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the desired intersection point are\n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105760 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba3182f3-3b70-5d65-bbb3-6a5e163b3a1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection of Two Circles Where One Is Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6d2cea6-2dfb-5470-bdb9-b125b8517442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6d2cea6-2dfb-5470-bdb9-b125b8517442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545558} :answer-field/id #uuid "44ef42ba-8904-590e-855b-cefdf44b2505" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545698} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac5c1f87-7097-5e55-8673-290f95420717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac5c1f87-7097-5e55-8673-290f95420717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545515} :answer-field/id #uuid "ece49f7d-e08d-5601-b02d-77ccc0ccc997" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "072c9b75-cb6d-571a-a32a-79cae44e115d" :question/math-academy-id "q-323087" :question/problem "![](images/ec/ec1381a50c493fce0f5017e706251c68c1a8a20ba5b68ef268d5905aa20772e6.png)\n\nThe circles $r=4\\sqrt{2}$ and $r=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ intersect at points $A$ and $B,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of the point $A?$ Express the polar angle $\\theta \\in [{0}^{∘},\\text{ }\\,{360}^{∘})$ in degrees.\n\n$(r,\\theta )=(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘})$" :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solving for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[{0}^{∘},\\text{ }\\,{360}^{∘}),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}={45}^{∘}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}={135}^{∘}.$ \n\nAlso, notice that $r=4\\sqrt{2}$ at both intersection points.\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the point $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(4\\sqrt{2},{135}^{∘}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545799 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105763 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "179ed9f0-7568-5cb0-8f06-626e1e494b62" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection Between a Circle and Another Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bddb17aa-bb31-5167-aec7-5f150d00bbdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bddb17aa-bb31-5167-aec7-5f150d00bbdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545546} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cafba33-0210-5ea7-a371-41e466aabf80" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545702} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "951aa75b-480f-5a6a-85e6-78d9055600b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "951aa75b-480f-5a6a-85e6-78d9055600b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545470} :answer-field/id #uuid "c14e8247-0744-5637-b0b4-5ca12a64d825" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d83a7e0-a78a-57e3-b959-267d62524fd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-324152" :question/problem "![](images/59/59ad56e7eae8e8598ebd511a7b499f35cd6a178405f8198831b23e8d5d47a513.png)\n\nThe circles $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $  intersect at points $O$ and $A,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of the point $A?$ Express the polar angle $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ in radians.\n\n$(r,\\theta )=(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we notice from the graph that the origin $O$ is an obvious first point of intersection. \n\nTo find the second point of intersection, we set the equations of the curves equal to each other and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the solution $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in the $1$st quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinate of the point $A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the point $A$ are \n\n \n\n$$\n(r,\\theta )=(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545870 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105763 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "179ed9f0-7568-5cb0-8f06-626e1e494b62" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection Between a Circle and Another Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b77ba13-51d9-5349-b156-c9977d72aec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b77ba13-51d9-5349-b156-c9977d72aec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545457} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed3bb5b2-9573-5648-9ac1-e2c7b129d109" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545783} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfbe5359-0657-5fe4-a2f2-94b550f4445f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfbe5359-0657-5fe4-a2f2-94b550f4445f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545613} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa646c97-4258-5a7b-a5e5-2be260e50b9a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eaf14619-5650-5c49-bb13-301ef3e6d48c" :question/math-academy-id "q-324164" :question/problem "![](images/51/515266ff58d998d660634860a07f26e65e0a2791a19a87e1a0f0cd8b8839c9b0.png)\n\nThe circles $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ intersect at points $O$ and $A,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of the point $A?$ Express the polar angle $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ in radians.\n\n$(r,\\theta )=(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we notice from the graph that the origin $O$ is an obvious first point of intersection. \n\nTo find the second point of intersection, we set the equations of the curves equal to each other and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the solution $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in the $1$st quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinate of the point $A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the point $A$ are \n\n$$\n(r,\\theta )=(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105763 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "179ed9f0-7568-5cb0-8f06-626e1e494b62" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection Between a Circle and Another Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26e6c9f3-21eb-55ea-9e4f-d0db71448f5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26e6c9f3-21eb-55ea-9e4f-d0db71448f5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545285} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fd15637-13c3-5b02-a6e6-7167a6179c28" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545694} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6d5555a-8e5b-5383-bfa8-67d65b8ccbe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6d5555a-8e5b-5383-bfa8-67d65b8ccbe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545628} :answer-field/id #uuid "564db509-6137-5b97-b4e7-601a8c1e8728" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545710} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddbc8538-19fd-5aae-827d-018fd0e5ba24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddbc8538-19fd-5aae-827d-018fd0e5ba24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545608} :answer-field/id #uuid "90685216-2313-5ba6-972a-4dedde848c5e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545741} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f429073a-dfbf-5e35-999b-44ef38ac203b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f429073a-dfbf-5e35-999b-44ef38ac203b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545649} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd614a72-9c5a-56ae-accf-fea2114cc61f" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c975a336-1c2a-5428-a315-180711c9bbb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-324172" :question/problem "![](images/ce/ce263ef057316e67e65b3adf1a5d66797c6a97c9bdcd33cae866d9111e523e10.png)\n\nThe curves $r=\\sqrt{3}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ intersect for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ at points $A$ and $B,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates $({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})$ and $({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})$ of the points $A$ and $B,$ respectively? Express the polar angles ${\\theta }_{A}$ and ${\\theta }_{B}$ in radians.\n\n$({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})=($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$),\\,$\n\n$({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})=($\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=\\sqrt{3}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{3}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0,2\\pi ),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinates of the intersection points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1} & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=-4\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {r}_{2} & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=-4\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates $({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})$ and $({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})$ of the points $A$ and $B,$ respectively, are\n\n$$\n({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})=(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})=(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545810 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105762 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64910a32-96b4-59c4-8f61-84e7c532e59d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection of a Limaçon and Another Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5524124b-024f-5686-b0d5-bd2a7c84b8ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5524124b-024f-5686-b0d5-bd2a7c84b8ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545359} :answer-field/id #uuid "5133b675-4392-570e-9804-043317b0b23f" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545707} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6860e7ff-a622-5842-b162-7d923e23d747" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6860e7ff-a622-5842-b162-7d923e23d747" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545397} :answer-field/id #uuid "6aa915cc-8a9a-52e8-bb02-a4b5a41ab146" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545717} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97bff314-7fab-5f05-a6b8-ebcad67eb541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97bff314-7fab-5f05-a6b8-ebcad67eb541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545472} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b154a83-e57a-5f7e-a3fd-bfc3e77d8375" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545725} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eccd2a69-62e6-5b27-b46d-fa183f6d99f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eccd2a69-62e6-5b27-b46d-fa183f6d99f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545640} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae6ca44a-c518-5e73-834b-2060d58c6c15" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d565dfb-3434-5111-8bf3-8db207613208" :question/math-academy-id "q-324188" :question/problem "![](images/40/40db66aea0bf6de657ea7fa673ece434c01fa81ae0f7c3664cf9bf1cf8fa02e0.png)\n\nThe polar curves $r=5-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ intersect  for $\\theta \\in [{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘})$ at points $A$ and $B,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates $({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})$ and $({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})$ of the points $A$ and $B,$ respectively? Express the polar angles ${\\theta }_{A}$ and ${\\theta }_{B}$ in degrees.\n\n$({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})=(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘})$\n$\\,and\\,$ \n\n$({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})=(${{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}${}^{∘})$" :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=5-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =4 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}={30}^{∘}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}={150}^{∘}.$ \n\nNow, we use the equation $r=1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinates of the intersection points: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1} & =1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{1}=1+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=3 \\\\ {r}_{2} & =1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{2}=1+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})=(3,{30}^{∘})\\,\\text{and }({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})=(3,{150}^{∘}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105762 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64910a32-96b4-59c4-8f61-84e7c532e59d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection of a Limaçon and Another Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd45d89c-18f2-5af8-af3a-72cc7f53e8f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd45d89c-18f2-5af8-af3a-72cc7f53e8f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545574} :answer-field/id #uuid "1789f9e8-fa16-5937-94c1-1a6d7f4887e0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545683} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54e10cfb-4665-5024-a9c4-1624bf7688ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54e10cfb-4665-5024-a9c4-1624bf7688ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545358} :answer-field/id #uuid "417ba2b3-92db-5f71-9eb2-99d78f38ddf7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a30f6995-a7bb-57e9-9de6-99d42c4d49a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-324194" :question/problem "The curves $r=2-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $r=-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ intersect at a single point in the third quadrant. Determine the polar coordinates of this point. Express the polar angle $\\theta $ in radians.\n\n$(r,\\theta )=(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=2-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2 & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0,2\\pi ),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the $\\theta $-coordinate of the point that we're interested in is $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3},$ since it lies in the third quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinate of our point:\n\n$$\nr=-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the desired intersection point are\n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(\\frac{7}{2},\\text{ }\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545804 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105760 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba3182f3-3b70-5d65-bbb3-6a5e163b3a1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection of Two Circles Where One Is Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e5b7fb9-337e-502c-9a40-f13cabc0424e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e5b7fb9-337e-502c-9a40-f13cabc0424e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545407} :answer-field/id #uuid "1438285a-c5cd-5a31-b715-b35536c03825" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545680} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f94ae38d-ef49-51f3-be98-ef3bdb8f6786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f94ae38d-ef49-51f3-be98-ef3bdb8f6786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545656} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e3de602-9b02-5e62-a90c-937f84dbf69b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545690} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b32348d-3761-5d58-acac-43ca4b05fbb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b32348d-3761-5d58-acac-43ca4b05fbb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545342} :answer-field/id #uuid "603497ab-4fdc-5c48-88e2-3ed5a2fed9cd" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545714} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3452fc1-db2a-5214-9f07-294a1399cd20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3452fc1-db2a-5214-9f07-294a1399cd20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545500} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c973c0b-c17f-575d-ba6c-439b72f6ffd9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2156ef1c-7aad-5ba1-895f-5a7013935e5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-325647" :question/problem "![](images/82/823ff7d93c17556b8587da62430c3168bbd6d49da43e88450f0804d83598f23f.png)\n\nThe circles $r=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $r=1$ intersect for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ at points $A$ and $B,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates $({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})$ and $({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})$ of the points $A$ and $B,$ respectively? Express the polar angles ${\\theta }_{A}$ and ${\\theta }_{B}$ in radians.\n\n$({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})=(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$),\\,$\n\n$({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})=(${{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0,2\\pi ),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$\n\nAlso, notice that $r=1$ at both intersection points.\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates $({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})$ and $({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})$ of the points $A$ and $B,$ respectively, are\n\n$$\n({r}_{A},{\\theta }_{A})=(1,\\frac{2\\pi }{3}),\\,({r}_{B},{\\theta }_{B})=(1,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105763 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "179ed9f0-7568-5cb0-8f06-626e1e494b62" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection Between a Circle and Another Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08ad9ece-6e98-5b75-949c-dce0b3e878c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},{55}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545218} {:answer/id #uuid "114f1305-c460-5d93-8f4a-d76eb9b5f8d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},{70}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545234} {:answer/id #uuid "421cfb40-fd75-524d-8050-661957d0bddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{4}{3},{60}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545323} {:answer/id #uuid "789e6d88-34e4-5b15-b0c8-2d19410bc047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{3},{60}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545429} {:answer/id #uuid "bb7f3cbf-3937-5e35-953e-49d18785c0fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},{60}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb7f3cbf-3937-5e35-953e-49d18785c0fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},{60}^{∘})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545539} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cb4aa30-610b-59c9-972c-ebd2569116be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed4d6b98-693b-555c-b503-e7cd998ff8be" :question/math-academy-id "q-41666" :question/problem "![](images/3c/3cd5ba688d516f05dbb9c8aa43626ec3194c384034ab612084da5dcc67a14ce6.png)\n\nThe circles $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $r=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ intersect at points $O$ and $A,$ as shown above. What are the polar coordinates of the point $A?$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we notice from the graph that the origin $O$ is an obvious first point of intersection.\n\nTo find the second point of intersection, we set the equations of the curves equal to each other and solve for $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the solution $\\theta ={60}^{∘}$ in the $1$st quadrant.\n\nNow, we use the equation $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinate of the point $A:$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡({60}^{∘}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(\\frac{3}{2},{60}^{∘}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105760 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba3182f3-3b70-5d65-bbb3-6a5e163b3a1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection of Two Circles Where One Is Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d56e221-abf1-5229-9caf-3e3b47a4855e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,\\frac{7\\pi }{6})$ and $(2,\\frac{11\\pi }{6})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545223} {:answer/id #uuid "16f8d075-a521-5559-bed6-a47dccce84fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,\\frac{6\\pi }{5})$ and $(2,\\frac{9\\pi }{5})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545242} {:answer/id #uuid "65f2c7e5-1aee-5f70-94f1-b52ebb032fd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{7\\pi }{6})$ and $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{11\\pi }{6})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545394} {:answer/id #uuid "8433a122-1303-5528-b61c-12e5dea05139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{4\\pi }{3})$ and $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545450} {:answer/id #uuid "c9223afd-b370-594f-bf98-95ec84abd306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{4})$ and $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{7\\pi }{4})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8433a122-1303-5528-b61c-12e5dea05139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{4\\pi }{3})$ and $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545450} :answer-field/id #uuid "aaacbcc3-32aa-5508-97fd-b5877b56f2cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d89c533-9b96-541f-8ca2-288a33c48339" :question/math-academy-id "q-42494" :question/problem "![](images/2e/2ee08e17ee0f474a675e83275c290a29a0f2814e5277694a5629282cfd861d56.png)\n\nFind the polar coordinates for the intersection points of the circles $r=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $r=\\sqrt{3},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[0,2\\pi ),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ .\n\nAlso, notice that $r=\\sqrt{3}$ at both intersection points.\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the points of intersection are \n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )=(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{4\\pi }{3})\\,$ and $\\,(r,\\theta )=(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}).$"}]}
